# events.jsonl is an append-only journal (SPEC-0002). Under parallel writing-session
# worktrees, two branches each append distinct rows; the built-in `union` merge
# driver keeps BOTH sides instead of raising a conflict. Consequence: physical line order
# is NO LONGER chronology — order by `ts` (SPEC-0002 §Concurrent-merge chronology). The
# `land` control-point drops duplicate rows post-union by canonical-object
# identity ( — serialization-robust; never merges two genuinely-distinct events).
events.jsonl merge=union

# tests/verify-durations.json is DELIBERATELY NOT declared here, and this comment is the record of
# why. It has the same problem the line above solves — it is machine-written, rewritten
# wholesale, and conflicts whenever two branches both rebuild it — but no committable git attribute
# fixes it:
# * `merge=union` is WRONG: it would interleave two JSON documents into one invalid file.
# * `merge=ours` is a SILENT NO-OP: a custom driver resolves only when `merge.ours.driver` exists
# in git CONFIG, config is never committed, and this machine has no merge driver configured at
# any level. The attribute would be present, the file would look fixed, and the next merge would
# conflict exactly as before.
# * unsetting (`-merge`) does not help either: git takes ours as the tentative result AND declares
# the merge conflicted, which is still a refused land.
# Git's only built-in low-level drivers are text/binary/union, so there is no keep-one-side driver to
# declare. Its resolution lives instead in `_DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS` (bin/lib/cli.py), the existing
# take-ours set for committed derived artifacts, which `_classify_land_merge_conflicts` applies at
# every seam that merges main into a branch (`land`'s update-from-main step, `land`'s in-lock post-ff
# fold, and `worktree sync`). Nothing above is changed by that entry: events.jsonl still unions.

# (SPEC-0190 rules 1+3): the journal's ARCHIVE SEGMENTS are physical units of the SAME
# logical journal — same line format, same parser, same append+union-merge discipline — so they must
# inherit `merge=union` for exactly the reason the live segment has it. The line above CANNOT cover
# them: it is a LITERAL PATH, and `archive/events-2026-08-16.jsonl` does not match it, so a segment
# would fall back to the default three-way merge and CONFLICT as soon as two branches both touch it
# — which rotation guarantees, since every landing branch rotates and each carries its own copy.
# `merge-non-union-conflict` is already the largest early-refusal class in this repo (62 land aborts
# in 7 days), so this is joining a measured bill, not guarding a hypothetical.
# The pattern is the definer's, not a re-spelling: `bin/lib/events.py#archive_glob` derives
# `events-*.jsonl` from the journal's own stem+suffix and `ARCHIVE_DIRNAME` gives the directory;
# tests/test_t11446_archive_admission.py asserts this literal still equals what they derive.
archive/events-*.jsonl merge=union
